  "summary": "Recent work applies deep learning and statistical models to denoise, integrate, and deconvolve single-cell data from sparse or pooled inputs.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is recovery of cell-level biological information from sparse, noisy, or aggregated measurements. SCR-MF pairs scRecover dropout detection with missForest imputation and is benchmarked on public and simulated scRNA-seq, while ZILLNB combines ZINB regression with InfoVAE-GAN latent factors to denoise scRNA-seq and improve cell typing and differential expression. Other items infer cell identity or composition from aggregated inputs: EPIC-unmix integrates single-cell reference profiles with bulk RNA-seq, and sex-dependent expression deconvolves pooled snRNA-seq with 93-95% cell-sex accuracy. A distinctive element is the use of cell-level context within conventional statistical or deep learning frameworks, as in XChrom's integration of sequence with single-cell context to predict chromatin accessibility in unseen regions and cells. A benchmark of 16 deep learning integration methods in a unified VAE framework adds a correlation-based loss and scIB-E metrics. ReMAC and LIONHEART are adjacent, applying methylation and cfDNA data to tumor problems.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-25"
